{
  "transcript": "Good morning Mr. Sharma. Please take a seat. What primary symptoms bring you to the eye clinic today?\nGiven the sudden onset of autopsy and vitreous floaters, we must evaluate for posterior vitreous detachment or a peripheral retinal tear. First, we will check the visual acuity, then perform dynamic retinoscopy and Goldman appellation tonometry to measure your intracolar pressure and screen.\nUnder slit-lamp bio-microscopy, your anterior segment, including the cornea and iridocorneal angle, is unremarkable. However, after installing tropic amide for pharmacological midraises, indirect ophthalmoscopy reveals radiographic retinal pigment\nand epithelium atrophy, drusen deposition, and mild localized macular edema. Is it age-related macular degeneration, doctor, or diabetic rectaloscopy? It is dry age-related muscular degeneration. Spectral domain optical coherence tomography shows disruption of the ellipsoid zone\nand the anterior subarpeal drusen, but no critical neovascularization or subretinal fluid. Furthermore, fluorescein angiography shows window defects without microneumatism or cotton wool spots. What is the prognosis and what are the symptoms?\nTherapies are available. Since there is no activate neovascular membrane, anti-VEGF intravenous injection, B-danivizumab are not indicated. We will initiate oral antioxidant R-side H2 supplement to retard disease progression and give you an abstract date for home surgery. If word conversion occurs, anionic therapy will be considered.\nUnderstood, doctor. I will start the ARDS-2 supplements and monitor you in my visual fields. Excellent. We will schedule a repeat OCD scan and wait for you to find us. Photography in three months. Take care.",
